WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.600
"Did you wash your hands?

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.100
With soap?"

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.600
If you want to know the secret purpose to
Amanda's key and other hidden clues about

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.920
future Saw movies then stick around to the
end of this video.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.460
[Intro]

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:19.490
Welcome to Things You Missed.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:22.199
Saw 3 brought back a lot of characters from
the first two movies.

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:26.779
We got to see Rigg, Kerry, Matthews and even
Adam Stanheight, who is played by the screenwriter,

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:27.779
Leigh Whannell.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:31.320
After writing the first two movies, Whannell
would make Saw III his final installment before

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.260
moving on to Dead Silence and Insidious.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.660
In one tweet, he hilariously claims that he'd
prefer Saw 3 as the end of the series.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.950
"To be honest, I think they should have ended
it at 3.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.780
I’ll admit it, I haven’t even seen Saw
5 or 6.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.030
People ask me questions about “Detective
Hoffman” and I don’t know what the f***

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.030
they’re talking about."

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.100
Which is really funny, because Leigh created
the character of Hoffman, he shows up in Saw

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.100
3.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.731
There are also a lot of clues in his story
that leave the third film up for a sequel.

00:00:54.731 --> 00:00:56.390
I wonder what Leigh Whannell would say to
that?

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.940
“I’m a liar?"

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.000
Let's get into the Things You Missed.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:07.340
Just like the first two movies, Saw 3 opens
with a light.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.420
This time, it's the flashlight that Detective
Matthews would try to use to make his way

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.960
out of The Nerve Gas House.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.120
The first proper trap of the movie is the
Classroom Trap, and we find out that this

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.060
one was never meant to be escapable, which
doesn't fit Jigsaw's pattern.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.540
I guess Amanda didn't spend enough time in
Jigsaw's workshop and spent a little too much

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.050
time at Build-a-Bear workshop, because she
didn't properly learn the rules for making

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.190
a Jigsaw game.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.320
Then, there's a curious line from Hoffman.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:37.220
“All he had to do is release himself from
each of the chains and walk out the door before

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:38.430
it went off.”

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.159
“All he had to do?”

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:43.120
At the time that line was written it could
have just been a red herring, but something

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.080
later on in the film makes me think it is
an intentional clue about Saw 4.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.030
It becomes clear to police that someone other
than Jigsaw is holding traps when they find

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.720
another inescapable one.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.560
No Kerry, that's not what I meant when I said
stream sniping.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.510
Last week when I covered Saw II, I mentioned
how some of the traps draw inspiration from

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.530
real life torture devices in history.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:12.620
The Angel Trap seems to be a post industrial
revolution version of The Blood Eagle, a Viking

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.959
execution ritual that severed the victims’
ribs from their spines.

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:19.700
The same thing happens to Detective Kerry
after reaching into a beaker of acid to get

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:20.700
the key.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.769
“All you have to do is reach in and take
it.”

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:25.810
Where have I heard that before?

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:27.329
“Takeeee itttt!”

00:02:27.329 --> 00:02:30.829
Towards the end of the movie, we see a prototype
in John’s work area that is never seen used

00:02:30.829 --> 00:02:34.670
on any victim in the series, but may have
also been inspired by a historical torture

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:38.349
mechanism, the Strappado, an invention of
the medeival Inquisition which left its victims

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:42.450
suspended by ropes for extended periods of
time to the point of body part dislocation

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.450
or death.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.909
There was a third torture device inspired
by real life in this film, but I'll come back

00:02:46.909 --> 00:02:48.599
to that later in the video.

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:52.019
After seeing the fate of Kerry, we’re introduced
to this movie’s main protagonist, a surgeon

00:02:52.019 --> 00:02:53.680
named Dr. Lynn Denlon.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.819
One of the big twists of this movie reveals
that she’s actually the wife of the man

00:02:56.819 --> 00:03:00.500
making his way through the trials at Gideon
Meat Packing Plant, however, if we look back

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.200
there are plenty of clues.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.239
When we see her at the hospital, she’s depressed,
and the only thing that ends up springing

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:08.010
her into action is when she sees that the
patient is a child.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.519
Lynn was unable to do anything to save her
and Jeff’s child Dylan, who was struck down

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:15.650
by a drunk driver, so she probably couldn’t
bear to see another child slip away.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:18.760
Then in her locker, there is a picture drawn
for her by her child, but what we don’t

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.489
know at the time is that it’s a keepsake
from her lost son Dylan.

00:03:21.489 --> 00:03:25.420
The drawing of the pig matches what was Dylan’s
favorite toy, a plush pig that looks like

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.829
it could be from The Other Mother in Coraline.

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:29.859
“We need a yes if you want to stay here.”

00:03:29.859 --> 00:03:32.629
“So sharp you won’t feel a th-- AUGH!!!”

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:35.640
Perhaps it should have been clear when Jeff
makes his way through his trials, and at every

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.330
step he is rewarded with a ripped piece of
this family photo.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:41.919
The mom is deliberately kept from us until
the end, moments before we discover that Lynn

00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:43.419
and Jeff are a married couple.

00:03:43.419 --> 00:03:46.599
But the hints about their relationship weren’t
the only windows into the events that would

00:03:46.599 --> 00:03:48.459
take place at the end of this movie.

00:03:48.459 --> 00:03:51.799
Another twist that takes place is the kidnapping
of Jeff and Lynn’s daughter Corbett to be

00:03:51.799 --> 00:03:54.019
used as motivation for a game for Jeff.

00:03:54.019 --> 00:03:57.680
In the scene where Jeff is kidnapped by Jigsaw’s
apprentice, there are also very short flashes

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.749
of Corbett’s face, suggesting that she too
was taken in.

00:04:00.749 --> 00:04:02.930
Jigsaw explains the rules of the game to Jeff.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:06.480
“You will have to face a series of tests.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.659
You will have to suffer to move forward through
each of them…

00:04:10.659 --> 00:04:15.599
You will finally come face to face with the
man responsible for the loss of your child.

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:19.129
That will be your ultimate test.

00:04:19.129 --> 00:04:21.479
Can you forgive him?”

00:04:21.479 --> 00:04:25.099
We assume that he’s talking about Timothy,
the drunk driver responsible for Dylan’s

00:04:25.099 --> 00:04:28.629
death, but when Jigsaw says ‘you will come
face to face with the man responsible for

00:04:28.629 --> 00:04:33.020
the loss of your child,’ he’s really talking
about himself, the man responsible for Corbett’s

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:34.020
kidnapping.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.190
Jeff’s final test is to try to forgive John
Kramer, who just put him through a whole day’s

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:38.190
worth of suffering.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:42.020
Corbett is locked away in an unknown location
inside Gideon, a part of Jigsaw’s plan that

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.729
is perhaps hinted at by this sketch in the
operating room of an eye looking through a

00:04:45.729 --> 00:04:46.729
keyhole.

00:04:46.729 --> 00:04:49.879
Could the key that Amanda wears throughout
the movie, a key that we never learn the purpose

00:04:49.879 --> 00:04:52.680
of, possibly be the key that unlocks Corbett’s
cell?

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:56.270
While following the pathways of Lynn and Jeff
have led us to some Things You Missed, the

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:59.840
biggest revelations will come when we take
a look at this game’s main test subject:

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:00.880
Amanda.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.841
The true surprise of Saw 3 was the deception
of Amanda's status as a facilitator of the

00:05:15.841 --> 00:05:17.259
game rather than a player.

00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:20.949
The final scene reveals that Jigsaw was really
looking at her when he began the game, but

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:22.940
the rules can apply to both Lynn and Amanda.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.580
"You are being tested.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:26.680
Your will is being tested.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.180
Your will to keep someone alive."

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:32.991
In a flashback, we see Amanda surviving the
reverse-bear trap from the first movie, after

00:05:32.991 --> 00:05:35.110
which Jigsaw takes her in for her "rebirth".

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:38.129
There are a couple items in Amanda's room
within the Gideon Meat Packing Plant that

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:42.720
symbolize this, like this Botticelli painting
titled the Birth of Venus, and a Jigsaw puzzle

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:43.720
piece.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:46.960
Amanda supposedly has the survival instinct
that Jigsaw victims lacked.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:50.810
The failed subjects had their piece taken
from them, while Amanda got to keep hers.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.949
Police figure out that the traps at the beginning
are unwinnable, and I mentioned back in my

00:05:53.949 --> 00:05:57.150
Saw 1 episode that I think Adam's chain was
tampered with as well.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:01.259
We see the bathroom game being set up in a
flashback in Saw 3, and Amanda tosses Adam's

00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:03.379
key into the bath while John is not looking.

00:06:03.379 --> 00:06:06.610
I think the key was supposed to be somewhere
where Adam had some chance to get it, giving

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.509
him the ability to roam around the bathroom by undoing his shackle.

00:06:09.509 --> 00:06:12.960
In the montage at the end, Amanda's placement
of the key is accompanied by the narration:

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:14.620
"your games were unwinnable".

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.700
“Your games were unwinnable…”

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:20.060
There's also a point when John is speaking
to Lynn Denlon about his moral values and

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:24.430
sheds light on the reason we would eventually
find out, for Amanda being tested once again.

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:29.710
"I don't condone murder and I despise murderers."

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:33.560
Which is a fine stance to have, but you have
to apply it to yourself as well.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.080
"I'm a murderer."

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.979
John should be holding himself accountable
for the deaths of the victims that he put

00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:41.750
into his many traps, so let's take a closer
look at those.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:44.030
If you ask me though, Jeff is going the wrong
way here.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.900
I'd be headed towards those fresh and frozen
beef products.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:50.550
When he arrives at the pig vat trap, the judge
just won't stop running his mouth, to the

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.439
point he started to sound like a talkative
video game NPC.

00:06:53.439 --> 00:06:54.439
“Hello?

00:06:54.439 --> 00:06:55.710
Help me, please!

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:57.410
Please help me!

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:59.110
I’m in here!

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:00.820
Please get me out of here.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:02.080
Help me I-- Hello?

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.160
I know you’re there, please, please help
me!

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:08.440
I-I can hear you, please, answer me!

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:09.580
Help me!

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:10.680
Please!

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:11.480
Help me!

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:13.100
Get me out of here, please, please.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.060
Get me out of here!

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:16.140
PLEASE!

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:17.640
Don’t just stand there, help m--”

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:21.200
“Who was that Cryptushka?”

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.020
“Telemarketer.”

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.860
This trap is clever though because it's more
of an emotional suffering that takes place,

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.860
whereas the others focus on physical pain.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.479
I think it's the only trap in the series so
far that's like that.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:32.380
It's also pretty metal.

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:39.880
*metal music*

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:42.000
Drowning in the guts of a dead rotted animal?

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.660
"I hate to say it but yeah.”

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:46.360
“Yeah, not to be contradictory but it’s
very metal.”

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:51.800
The challenge is for Jeff to burn his late
son's possessions to obtain a key that will

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.090
free the judge from being drowned in pig guts.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:56.840
The key is hidden in Dylan's favorite toy,
which was a pig.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:00.550
As I mentioned before, pigs represent rebirth,
because the Year of the Pig in the Chinese

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:03.249
Zodiac is associated with the concept of being
reborn.

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:07.819
For Jigsaw, being reborn is the idea of leaving
your past life behind, and coming to appreciate

00:08:07.819 --> 00:08:09.960
and cherish the joy being alive.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:12.110
So that's why there are pigs all over this
movie.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:14.770
I already mentioned Dylan's drawing inside
of Lynn's locker.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:18.689
There's also these little porkers up here,
the pig masks worn by John's disciples, these

00:08:18.689 --> 00:08:22.379
guys in the operation room and the pig hidden
at the top of Jeff's study.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:23.840
Let's move on to the next trap.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:28.580
What was the point of that?

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:32.200
This trap is another example of a trap that's
based off of a real world torture device from

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.670
history, but this one you're probably familiar
with.

00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:38.530
The cross has long been a religious symbol
for some, but it gets its shape from the ancient

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:40.640
punishment known as Crucifixion.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.210
Looking at The Rack makes me think of a more
technologically advanced crucifix.

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:47.840
I’ve talked a little bit about how John
tries to make his following very cult like,

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:51.170
but he doesn’t just draw influence from
one religion, there are also what I believe

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:54.820
are Buddhist figures with him by his deathbed,
and I’ve also heard the spirals that he’s

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:57.660
incorporated into much of his work have connections
to Buddhism as well.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:00.950
I think that the imagery connected to the
cult of Jigsaw doesn’t stop with religion

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:04.520
though, he’s also taken ideas from other
cultures, like how we the Chinese concept

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.340
of Yin and Yang referenced right here, and
he also has illustrations of the solar system

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:11.670
because there are certain cultures who put
a lot of significance on the solar calendar.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:15.640
I see the Jigsaw cult as kind of a melting
pot of different religions and cultures with

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:18.020
an emphasis on suffering to find enlightenment.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:22.080
Jigsaw wears robes resembling those of a monk
in the scene where Amanda is reborn.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.940
The room is lit by candle light, the music
that plays is titled Baptism in the Official

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:26.960
Score.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:38.660
*piano music with chanting*

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.430
One more note on the Rack though.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.590
Rather than having the feet together, like
you typically see on a crucifix, the feet

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:47.960
are apart, like the second position seen on
Leonardo Da Vinci’s famous drawing The Vitruvian

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:51.960
Man, which happens to be posted up all over
the operating room where that part of John’s

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.040
game takes place.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.040
Right about now, you’re probably thinking
that “this Jigsaw guy is pretty sharp, the

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.930
kind of guy that’s so smart he wears his
watch on the inside of his wrist.”

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.370
Only problem is, you might want to turn on
your camera there, K Boomer?

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:07.070
Unlike Saw 2, there’s only one horror reference
I caught in this one, and keep in mind when

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:11.050
you see this Annabelle doll, this was years
before The Conjuring or Annabelle came out,

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:14.600
so this is a reference to the actual real
life creepy doll that the movies are based

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:15.600
on... if that.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.850
I have done episodes on all the Annabelle
movies, but I’ve yet to cover the mainline

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:19.890
Conjuring trilogy.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:24.310
Though that doesn’t sound like a bad idea
as a franchise to do after I cover Saw.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:28.410
Now that may or may not be a hint at what’s
coming next on this channel, but one person

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:32.320
who definitely left hints at what would follow,
despite saying it should have ended after

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.280
Saw 3, was the screenwriter Leigh Whannell.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:38.060
The first hint was early on in the movie when
John Kramer discusses his relationship with

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:39.060
Dr. Gordon.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:42.850
“I was his patient and he was mine.”

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:44.350
Interesting choice of words.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:48.260
But it also seems like the original plan for
Jeff was for him to survive and play another

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:49.520
game in Saw 4.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.260
As I discussed, the ending alludes to a game
where he has to save his daughter.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:57.620
“I am the person responsible for the loss
of your child.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:03.980
I am the only person who knows where your
daughter is.”

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:07.410
Jigsaw also gives a cryptic warning, telling
Jeff that it would be dangerous to try to

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:09.290
move his dying wife.

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:15.950
"Though you may not see any threat, there
are threats all around you."

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:19.450
In Saw 3, we don’t really have any idea
what he means by this, but in Saw 4 we would

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:22.130
meet the threats that approach just beyond
that door.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:25.240
Obviously the storyline did change in the
year that would follow, to where Jeff was

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:28.620
no longer the focus, but you’re not off
the hook just yet Leigh Whannell.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:29.620
Remember that tweet?

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:33.701
I find it a little suspicious that Hoffman
was introduced in Saw 3, left as one of the

00:11:33.701 --> 00:11:37.940
only survivors, and that this shot, which
has nothing to do with Saw 3, was added to

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:39.980
kickstart the beginning of Saw 4.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:44.010
So if you want to hear about all the new Things
You Missed that Saw 4 had to offer, then remember

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:49.170
to subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every
week, ring that deathbell for notifications

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:51.560
and I’ll see you in the next one.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:52.990
Assuming we both survive.

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:55.040
And wash your hands.

